        ]
      },
      "text": "A planet signifies houses 6 and 11 but not 8 or 12. Such a planet brings profit at cards and like games when the Moon runs in its star and sub; planets that signify 8 or 12 are to be avoided.",
      "tone": "good",
      "cite": "B5 p. 231"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-140",
      "topic": "Self and temperament",
      "when": {
        "conj": [
          "Mo",
          "Sa"
        ]
      },
      "text": "The Moon and Saturn share a sign (“Punarphoo”). The first attempt at a matter tends to fail or be delayed; success comes at a later try.",
      "tone": "mixed",
      "cite": "B5 p. 288"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-001",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Transit is the “second hand” of the dasa clock: the dasa is the day, the bhukti the hour, the antara the minute. A transit only times what the running periods already promise; followed the KP way, the two agree.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 p. 26"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-005+KP5-006",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Judge a transit against the exact Lagna and all twelve cusps first, then the Moon, Sun and other planets. A transit over the natal Moon or Sun touches the houses that luminary owns in this chart (the cusps that fall in its own sign), not the native as a whole.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 15–21, 206–207"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-040+KP5-041",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Each star is a room stocked with the houses its lord signifies. A planet passing through it offers those houses: the star lord shows what happens, the transiting planet the source, and the sub lord, by its houses, whether it happens and whether it is good.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 190–195, 208–211"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-042",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "In KP the sign and house a planet transits do not matter. What counts is the transit of the significators through the star and sub of the significators of the matter.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 p. 365"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-043+KP5-044+KP5-045",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Entering a favourable star is not yet the event; it comes when the planet reaches the sub of a significator of the matter. Each sub on the way marks the next stage, and a sub lord signifying the opposite houses reverses the result while the planet stays in it.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 192, 211, 220"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-060+KP5-063",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "A matter comes when the zones whose sign, star and sub lords are the running dasa, bhukti and antara lords are transited by those lords, the Sun (the month), the Moon (the day) and the Lagna (the minute). Jupiter and Saturn in a good sub give long windows.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 195–197, 218, 365"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-064",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "For transit timing the Lagna is cast for the place where the native is at the time, not the birth place, unless the native still lives there.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 p. 196"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-048",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Several transits act at once and none cancels another: a gain shown by one planet and a bill shown by another can fall on the same day.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 312–313"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-050+KP5-051",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "When a slow planet turns back through subs it has already crossed, the results of those subs repeat. Retrograde in a bad sub worsens the trouble; in a good sub gains come but smaller. The time it stands still is the most intense.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 318–321, 325, 334–335"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-080+KP5-084",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "For gains and success in contests, houses 1, 2, 3, 6, 10 and 11 help and 4, 5, 7, 8, 9 and 12 hinder. The 5th gives the urge to speculate, not the profit (it is the 11th from the rival’s 7th); judge gain by 2, 6 and 11.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 22, 237, 247–248"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-004+KP5-150",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Tradition judges transits by counting signs from the natal Moon sign (Gochara, with a fixed table for each planet). Krishnamurti rejects this: people of the same star meet opposite results on one day, and sign counting ignores the real distance.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 10–12, 26"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-130+KP5-131",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Tradition holds the 7½ years of Sade Sati (Saturn in the 12th, 1st and 2nd sign from the Moon) are bad. Krishnamurti rejects this: many marry, get jobs or win prizes in it. Read instead the stars Saturn crosses and the houses their lords signify.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 281, 292–299"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-133+KP5-135+KP5-136",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Tradition fears Ashtama Sani (Saturn 8th from the Moon sign) and Chandrashtama (the Moon 8th from it, about 2¼ days a month). Krishnamurti rejects both: results follow the star and sub lords of the zone transited, and people win and marry on such days.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 277–290"
    },
    {
      "id": "KP5-137+KP5-138",
      "topic": "Principles of judgement",
      "when": {
        "always": true
      },
      "text": "Tradition holds that marriage needs Gurubalam (Jupiter 2nd, 5th, 7th, 9th or 11th from the Moon sign) and fears Jupiter in the 3rd from it. Krishnamurti rejects both: many marry and win with Jupiter in the 3rd.",
      "tone": "info",
      "cite": "B5 pp. 12, 283, 293, 295, 363"
    }
  ]
}
